    <section id="plan" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">02</p>
      <h2>Pick a plan</h2>
      <p>Focused decks do best in singleton. Pick one way to win, then make sure most of your hundred cards push toward it. These are the families you'll meet at a Canlander table.</p>
      <div class="archetypes">
        {%- for a in archetypes %}
        <article class="arch">
          <div class="arch__art" style="background-image:url('{{ a.art | cardImg('art_crop') }}')" role="img" aria-label="{{ a.art }} art"></div>
          <div class="arch__body">
            <p class="arch__tag mono">{{ a.tag }}</p>
            <h3 class="arch__name">{{ a.name }}</h3>
            <p>{{ a.how }}</p>
            <p class="arch__meta"><span class="mono">Points</span> {{ a.pts }}</p>
            <p class="arch__meta"><span class="mono">Look for</span> {{ a.keys }}</p>
          </div>
        </article>
        {%- endfor %}
      </div>
      <p>Not sure which suits you? Borrow a list from each family, play a few games on Cockatrice, and see which one you enjoy.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="mana" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">03</p>
      <h2>Build the mana</h2>
      <h3>You need fewer lands than you think</h3>
      <p>New players often register 36 to 40 lands. Competitive Canlander decks play far fewer, because the games are one-on-one, curves are low, and cheap cantrips like Brainstorm, Ponder and Preordain dig for land drops.</p>
      <div class="stat">
        {%- set ls = decks.decks | landStats %}
        <p class="stat__big">{{ ls.lo }}–{{ ls.hi }}</p>
        <p class="stat__label">Lands in most decks on the rotation. Median {{ ls.median }}, often plus a modal double-faced spell or two.</p>
      </div>
      <p>A sensible starting point is <b>34 lands</b> for midrange, <b>32–33</b> for low-curve aggro and tempo, and <b>35–36</b> for control, ramp and decks with expensive top ends. If you run three or more fast-mana pieces (Moxen, Sol Ring, Mana Crypt, Ancient Tomb), shave a land.</p>
      <h3>Expect your mana to be attacked</h3>
      <p>Mana denial is everywhere in Canlander, and it decides a lot of games. Plan for it from the first card you add. Most of it comes in three forms:</p>
      <dl class="rules-table">
        <div><dt>Blood Moon effects</dt><dd>Blood Moon, Magus of the Moon and friends turn every nonbasic land into a Mountain. A greedy three-colour mana base can lose on the spot.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Search hate</dt><dd>Aven Mindcensor, Leonin Arbiter, Opposition Agent and similar cards punish fetchlands and tutors. Crack fetches early when you can.</dd></div>
        <div><dt>Land destruction</dt><dd>Strip Mine and Wasteland are in almost every deck that can play them, and they love hitting your only source of a colour or your Urza's Saga.</dd></div>
      </dl>
      <p>The answer is in how you build. Play basics of your main colour, keep the colour count low, and don't lean on one land for a whole colour. Plenty of decks play these cards themselves, so consider which of them your deck wants too.</p>
      <h3>Play the best lands you can</h3>
      <p>A hundred-card deck has room for every fetchland, original dual and shockland in your colours, and you should play them. Fetches also feed Wrenn and Six, delirium and landfall. Just leave room for the basics from the section above.</p>
      <h3>Two or three colours is a great place to start</h3>
      <p>Fewer colours means smoother mana and more consistent games. The council has said it wants to <a href="https://canadianhighlander.ca/2025/03/26/2025-year-start-points-update-survey-report/">“promote lower color decks over four color decks”</a>, and the points list has been nudged that way. For a first deck, two colours with a light splash is the easiest to pilot.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="hundred" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">04</p>
      <h2>Fill the hundred</h2>
      <h3>Think in roles</h3>
      <p>In singleton you get one Lightning Bolt, plus Chain Lightning, Burst Lightning, Fireblast and Lava Dart. Work out the roles your deck needs (removal, card draw, threats and so on) and play enough cards in each role that you'll find one when you need it.</p>
      <div class="skeleton">
        <p class="skeleton__title mono">A starting skeleton for midrange</p>
        <table>
          <thead><tr><th>Job</th><th>Slots</th><th>Examples</th></tr></thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr><td>Lands</td><td>33–35</td><td>Fetches, duals, shocks, a few basics, utility lands</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Cheap interaction</td><td>14–18</td><td>Swords to Plowshares, Lightning Bolt, Fatal Push, Counterspell, Thoughtseize</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Card advantage &amp; selection</td><td>8–12</td><td>Brainstorm, Ponder, Fact or Fiction, Fable of the Mirror-Breaker</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Threats</td><td>20–25</td><td>Ragavan, Psychic Frog, Orcish Bowmasters, Murktide Regent</td></tr>
            <tr><td>Flexible answers &amp; hate</td><td>6–10</td><td>Kolaghan's Command, Abrupt Decay, Council's Judgment, Force of Will</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
        <p class="skeleton__note">Ranges, not rules. Aggro trades card advantage for more threats; control does the opposite.</p>
      </div>
      <h3>Pick cards that are good in every matchup</h3>
      <p>Flexible answers are your best friends: Kolaghan's Command, Abrupt Decay, Assassin's Trophy, Council's Judgment, Prismatic Ending. Force of Will costs zero points and answers anything. Narrow hate cards are great when your local metagame calls for them.</p>
      <h3>Keep the curve low</h3>
      <p>A one-on-one game at twenty life rewards tempo. Most competitive lists keep the bulk of their spells at one to three mana and only a handful above four. Make your expensive cards count: the best ones win the game or draw you cards.</p>
    </section>

    <section id="tune" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">05</p>
      <h2>Tune it</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><b>Build it on Moxfield.</b> Its Canadian Highlander format totals your points and flags an illegal list for you.</li>
        <li><b>Goldfish opening hands.</b> Draw twenty seven-card hands. If you'd mulligan more than a few, fix the mana before anything else.</li>
        <li><b>Play twenty games on Cockatrice.</b> Note which cards you'd most like to upgrade. Those are the first ones to swap.</li>
        <li><b>Change ten cards at a time</b> so you can feel the difference.</li>
        <li><b>Watch the points calendar.</b> Updates land roughly every quarter, so give your list a quick check after each one.</li>
      </ul>
    </section>

    <section id="checklist" class="docs__section">
      <p class="docs__num mono">06</p>
      <h2>Before you sleeve up</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Exactly 100 cards, one of each, basics excepted.</li>
        <li>All ten points spent, checked against <a href="{{ site.official.points }}">today's list</a>.</li>
        <li>Your points help your game plan.</li>
        <li>32–36 lands, adjusted for fast mana and curve.</li>
        <li>At least fourteen pieces of cheap interaction.</li>
        <li>Enough basics to survive Blood Moon, Wasteland and search hate.</li>
        <li>Every card is live against every deck.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Then go play some games. You'll learn more from ten games than from any guide.</p>
    </section>

  </article>
